def longest_bitonic_length(nums: list[int]) -> int:
    if not nums:
        return 0

    n = len(nums)
    inc = [1] * n  # longest increasing subsequence ending at index i
    dec = [1] * n  # longest decreasing subsequence starting at index i

    # compute longest increasing subsequence
    for i in range(1, n):
        for j in range(i):
            if nums[i] > nums[j]:
                inc[i] = max(inc[i], inc[j] + 1)

    # compute longest decreasing subsequence
    for i in range(n - 2, -1, -1):
        for j in range(n - 1, i, -1):
            if nums[i] > nums[j]:
                dec[i] = max(dec[i], dec[j] + 1)

    # longest bitonic subsequence is maximum of all increasing and decreasing subsequences
    return max(inc[i] + dec[i] - 1 for i in range(n))
```

This function first computes the longest increasing subsequence (`inc`) and the longest decreasing subsequence (`dec`) for each index in `nums`. Then it returns the maximum sum of the longest increasing and decreasing subsequences minus 1 (to exclude the common element in the bitonic subsequence).

